WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.110
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Sheila Alecky, who is a reporter with

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:10.840
ICG and also coordinates the organization's
partners in Asia and Western Europe.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.730
Thanks so much for talking to us today.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.110
Thank you.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:19.180
So my audience has certainly heard of the
Pandora papers we've reported.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.580
I don't know about extensively, but we've
reported about the Pandora papers for people

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.940
who maybe aren't familiar with what these
documents are.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.189
How do you generally summarize what the documents
are?

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:36.100
Speaker 4: Well, I would say that Pandora
Papers is an investigation based on a leak

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:43.440
of about 12 million documents, confidential
financial records coming from 14 different

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.579
financial service providers from around the
world, from the most secretive, secretive

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:54.150
jurisdictions including Seychelles, Cyprus,
BVI and so forth.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:01:01.829
And what these documents tell us is how the
offshore financial system works, but not just

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:02.829
that.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:08.869
Also how the global economy in a way works
because the system that thrives on secrecy

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:15.490
and anonymity is basically a big part of the
global economy right now.

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:24.240
Speaker 1: And some of the biggest headlines
related to individual prominent individuals,

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:30.880
politicians, elected officials, functionaries
of other kinds, well-known people.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:37.130
I don't know if secretly is the right word,
but certainly behind the scenes using a lot

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:42.429
of these other other mechanisms and instruments
for their own personal finances.

00:01:42.429 --> 00:01:43.679
Is that a fair assessment?

00:01:43.679 --> 00:01:45.649
Speaker 4: I think so.

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:52.549
What we found was that more than 300 politicians,
including more than 30 current and former

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:59.700
country leaders, used shell companies and
offshore trusts to hide wealth from their

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.670
own citizens as well.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:09.719
So public elected officials, such as the President
of Kenya, the King of Jordan and the then

00:02:09.719 --> 00:02:17.569
prime minister, Texu public and other prominent
politicians use these kind of structures to

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:21.989
to hide wealth or buy real estate around the
world.

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:25.291
And obviously, we have to be careful here.

00:02:25.291 --> 00:02:28.760
We don't want to say that all of this is illegal.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.989
Much of these actually is legal and legitimate.

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:39.290
But the problem here is actually that it allows
people to avoid scrutiny.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:48.200
And so it can be used by husbands who don't
want their divorces to know about their assets,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.800
as well as criminals who don't want authorities
to know about their assets.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:58.719
Speaker 1: How does the ICJ obtain these documents
to the extent that you can talk to us about

00:02:58.719 --> 00:02:59.719
the process?

00:02:59.719 --> 00:03:03.390
Speaker 4: And yes, we don't comment on our
sources.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:11.230
We obtain these documents, and what we did
initially was a fact checking most of them

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:14.640
and trying to figure out whether they were
actually whether they were accurate and where

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:21.609
they were fake fake documents and the way
we do this is a process that we have learned

00:03:21.609 --> 00:03:26.670
to do over time because this is not the first
offshore financial project that we do.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:31.750
We obviously check the leaked documents against
public records.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:37.530
We always involve the journalists from many
different countries who have the expertize

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:43.010
and the sources who allow us also to verify
the information, whether it's true or not.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.629
And as you know, we don't publish.

00:03:44.629 --> 00:03:51.890
We don't publish the whole dataset because
there are confidential information and also,

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:54.650
you know, information that it may not be true.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:04:05.069
So we try to go public, publish on these stories
that we are able to verify and and also comment

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:06.069
for.

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:09.829
Speaker 1: Yeah, I wanted that was going to
be my next question about asking for comment.

00:04:09.829 --> 00:04:16.400
In many types of stories, when someone is
written about you, ask that person for a comment,

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.400
there's a deadline.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.970
They have the opportunity to say something
or to say no comment or to not even respond

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.210
at all.

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:30.200
Here the story is really the documents, and
if the documents are accurate, the concept

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.620
of a comment is a little bit different, right?

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.169
Because it's basically.

00:04:34.169 --> 00:04:37.660
What exactly are we asking for when we ask
for comment about a document?

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:42.810
Speaker 4: Well, we want to know the context,
because sometimes you don't have the story

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:44.000
in one document.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.199
You may know that one person set up a company
and that's it.

00:04:48.199 --> 00:04:52.590
You may know what it is for sometimes you
don't even know what the purpose of this company

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:53.590
is.

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:54.590
So obviously, we want to know.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:56.169
And we also want to know the purpose.

00:04:56.169 --> 00:05:01.099
For example, if it was for tax reasons or
not.

00:05:01.099 --> 00:05:07.750
And then obviously, you know, we also want
to just give the opportunity to anybody to

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:10.060
to say to have their say.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:18.240
And I say alone, we are about 15 reporters
and editors on staff, and we sent more than

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:24.440
400 comments requests just for this investigation,
excluding all the requests that our partners

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:25.650
have sent.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:32.990
So every name that is ours on our story, we
will will be fact checked and they will have

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:34.370
the opportunity to comment.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:37.370
Speaker 1: Now, out of the 400, how many chose
to comment?

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:43.440
Speaker 4: Now I don't know the exact percentage,
but I must say that what I've noticed is that

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:49.760
over time, people, especially politicians,
have developed a very good strategy and most

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.610
of them did not respond or did not respond
adequately.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:06:00.310
Or they chose not to respond to our questions
and wait for after publication to go on their

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:04.930
social media and Facebook and, you know, give
their own opinion.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.940
They're directly to their followers.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:14.409
Speaker 1: You mentioned that a lot of what
is revealed is not against the law, but it

00:06:14.409 --> 00:06:21.080
can be used in ways to circumvent reporting
requirements, or there's a lot of different

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.550
ways in which these mechanisms can be used.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:30.670
How much of of the investigation is talking
to lawyers about, is there anything illegal

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:32.350
here and highlighting the illegal?

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.270
Speaker 4: A lot.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:37.250
A lot of because we are journalists, we are
not experts.

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:39.259
Obviously, we've been doing this for many
years.

00:06:39.259 --> 00:06:44.460
So there are some basic concepts and knowledge
that we have.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:51.169
But at the same time, you also want to be
sure that you know you are up to date with

00:06:51.169 --> 00:06:54.599
the most recent legislation or regulation,
et cetera.

00:06:54.599 --> 00:07:01.280
And also here we talk about hundreds of countries,
so each country has its specific regulation.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:07.870
And after Panama Papers, which is an investigation
we did in 2016, some laws changed.

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:13.280
So you also want to be sure that what you're
reporting now is obviously up to date.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:24.870
So yes, we we love experts and they are they
they always help us understand the context.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:29.940
Sometimes when we have teams reporting teams
from many different countries, we also have

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:34.990
experts from many different countries who
kind of tell us what's going on.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:38.599
Speaker 1: So for this, I want to give a little
context for my next question.

00:07:38.599 --> 00:07:42.530
In the United States, certainly, and I think
this happens in other countries, too, whenever

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:44.780
there is a leak of information.

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:51.889
If you like the content of the leak, you focus
on the content and you ignore how the documents

00:07:51.889 --> 00:07:53.060
were obtained.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:57.889
If you don't like the content of the leak,
you ignore the content and you say, How dare

00:07:57.889 --> 00:07:59.410
they get these private documents?

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:03.550
You focus only on how it was obtained when
we're talking about banking records.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:08.870
I mean, there certainly are privacy laws,
and different countries have different rules,

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:12.979
the same as with medical records, with with
banking information.

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:17.120
How does the ICJ think about that?

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:24.099
At the end of the day, these documents are
presumably documents that are believed to

00:08:24.099 --> 00:08:29.090
be private documents and how what is the sort
of ethical approach to that?

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:36.390
Speaker 4: So first of all, whenever we publish
a name or a story, we always ask whether it's

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:37.730
in the public interest.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:42.830
So I'm not going to publish my neighbors banking
records and public cares about that.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:44.500
But I do care about a politician.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:45.500
I do.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:50.270
We care also about a powerful business person
who may have a political ties or any impact

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:52.540
on a community.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:55.100
Obviously, I do care about criminals.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:09:01.920
So that's some ethical discussion that we,
you know, we have always have before publishing

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.020
anything.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:09.460
And then again, you go and ask for comment
so you don't do it behind the back, you know,

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:14.320
like you always try to confront the people
that you're writing about.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:17.410
So it's not a surprise.

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:23.960
And when I say we go to court for comment,
we have, you know, we we ask about a list

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:30.220
of five weeks before publication so that we
can always ask at least three times calling.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:38.170
I have sent, for example, last time I sent
an email, not an email, a registered mail.

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:39.410
To Myanmar.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:44.440
You know, because there was only a physical
address that I could use and you try whatever

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.160
you can, are there.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:52.290
Speaker 1: Is this the type of investigation
where for your team there are physical security

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:55.140
concerns for the reporters that are doing
the investigating?

00:09:55.140 --> 00:10:02.450
Speaker 4: So lucky, I see this team, and
most of it is based in the US or in Europe,

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:08.590
but of course we work with journalists from
around the world and from Russia, Hong Kong,

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:10.880
for example, Arab countries.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:13.030
And we have a lot of also Latin America.

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:19.200
And many Latin American countries have press
freedom issues so more than they icj stuff.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.620
Obviously, we're always concerned for our
partners and we work with them every day.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:24.900
We always in touch.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:29.380
So if there is an issue for them, we try to
help them.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:34.720
For example, one thing that we do is if a
partner cannot publish a story in their own

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:39.940
country, there may be a partner in a neighboring
country that can publish that story for them

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:45.890
so that the person or the reporters in trouble
can report on something that has already been

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:48.570
reported without exposing themselves.

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:49.570
So we try to.

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:55.950
So that's why collaboration is great because
it also help protect each other and especially

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:59.580
people in more vulnerable or vulnerable situations.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:05.520
Speaker 1: Whenever there are these these
big leaks and revelations, there's the question

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:12.820
of, OK, so what is the ideal consequence or
outcome with, you know, you look at the Edward

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:18.230
Snowden NSA leaks, and there are a lot of
people who said, well, the the outcome should

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:22.300
be now that people have been made aware of
these techniques.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:23.540
Those should be changed.

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:24.670
They should be made illegal.

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:27.350
Or, you know, there's kind of like a clear
path.

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:28.350
What's the goal here?

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:35.090
Is it sort of international tax and finance
policy reform or what would be the outcome

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:36.690
that would be an ideal outcome?

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:42.040
Speaker 4: Well, certainly transparency is
something that we would hope to have more

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:43.040
around the world.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:50.280
You know, and after Panama Papers, for example,
some countries introduced the laws to, for

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:55.320
example, reports record of beneficial ownership
and so forth.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:59.250
And every time, obviously, that we do a project
like this, new loopholes emerge.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:03.540
For example, this time something interesting
had to do with the US.

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:08.610
And what we learned was that while the smaller
jurisdictions, for example, like Bahamas,

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:14.600
were trying to improve their own system, huge
economies like the US, they were actually

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:19.600
becoming the go to place for people to hide
money.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:27.580
And so this time we really hope that if the
US that actually step up and improve their

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:32.170
own regulations
Speaker 1: when it comes to people like Elton

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:38.020
John, Jackie Chan, you know, Shakira named
named in the papers.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:41.800
Talk to me about the public interest there,
and to be clear, I'm not saying there is no

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:43.050
public interest.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:48.300
It seems different than an elected official
that might influence policy, however.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.080
So I'm curious about the public interest when
it comes to entertainers.

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:54.980
You know,
Speaker 4: I understand that there is a difference

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:58.790
between a politician, an elected official
and a pop star.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:04.020
But at the same time, these people have a
lot of influence, even if it's just, you know,

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:09.320
on people a lot of impact on on everybody.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:13.850
And so they kind of like they they chose a
public life in a way.

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:22.000
And so obviously the their their finances,
if obviously if we find something that is

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.650
worth reporting, then that can be put under
scrutiny.

00:13:26.650 --> 00:13:31.980
At the same time, we always go and ask for
comment.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:38.690
And so, for example, the where a few of these
celebrities that came back saying that they

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:45.220
had done nothing wrong and which we also reported
like models, et cetera.

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:51.720
But you know, this is a system where everybody
that we want people to understand why it is

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:52.720
significant.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:59.920
At the same time, whether it while it is legitimate,
in some cases, you know, you still have tax

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.280
revenues that are lost.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:05.290
So we want the public to be informed.

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:11.110
And whether it's a politician or a celebrity
that contributes to the system, I think it's

00:14:11.110 --> 00:14:13.010
still worth talking about it.

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:20.020
Speaker 1: Well, it's an incredible investigation,
and I encourage people in my audience to go

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:23.880
to the ICIJ website and there's so, so much
documentation there.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:28.230
We've been speaking with Sheila Allecha, who
is a reporter with the ICJ.

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.530
I really appreciate your time today.

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:31.760
Thanks so much for talking to us.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:32.740
Speaker 4: Thank you.

